Rebuilt the two demo profiles out of data instead of Python. Each site's settings are its profile's `registry` XML, its principals and content are a `plone.exportimport` payload, and what is left in each handler is the handful of URLs that cannot be stated as data: there are two demo deployments, they do not agree on them, and XML cannot read an environment variable.

Which is the point. `api.user.create` with four keyword arguments describes one user and nothing else; the same user as JSON is the shape the exporter writes, so the way to change what the demo ships is to configure a site, export it, and commit what comes out. The payload grows without either handler growing — the importer reads every file it recognises and reports "no data to import" for the rest.

The demo user's password is the one field that cannot come from the payload, since an export carries the hash. It is asserted against that hash by the test suite, so re-exporting after changing it cannot leave the documented password quietly wrong. The opt-in guard stays on the relying party's handler, which is the profile that puts a published client secret into a site; the identity provider's no longer calls it, because refusing to create a demo user whose password is in the same public repository protects nobody.

`tests/demo` pins that the demo registry XML survives the real importer. The file spent a day in a shape GenericSetup rejects outright — `<records prefix="...">` with no `interface` — which raises before a single record is applied and takes the sections above it down too. @ericof
